<Doc
  title="Overview"
  description="mcpd is a free, open-source daemon for AI agents: durable runs held between steps, and observability for every step. One process, storage compiled in, OpenTelemetry in, no framework to adopt."
  lede="A process you run beside your agent, not a library your agent is built around. It holds the run between steps and records each one. This page is the shape of the whole thing in one read."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>One process</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd does two things with one process. It holds a durable run between its
    steps: the cursor, the retries, the timers and the waits, while your own
    code, in any language, does the work inside each step. And it receives what
    your agent did, stores it, and gives it back to you as runs you can read.
    It is one binary with SQLite linked into it. There is no database to stand
    up, no collector to run alongside it, and no account anywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    The daemon listens on port <code>4317</code> and serves five things from
    that single port:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      OTLP ingest at <code>/v1/traces</code>, <code>/v1/logs</code> and
      <code>/v1/metrics</code>, so an existing OpenTelemetry setup needs no new
      code.
    </li>
    <li>
      Native ingest at <code>/v1/ingest</code>, which carries the parts of the
      model OTLP has no place for, including scores and human feedback.
    </li>
    <li>
      The read API under <code>/v1/api</code>, which the interface and the SDKs
      both use. There is no private API behind the one you get.
    </li>
    <li>
      The run surface under <code>/v1/api/runs</code>, <code>/v1/api/events</code>
      and <code>/v1/api/steps</code>: definitions, runs, the events that answer
      a wait, and the protocol a worker speaks. See
      <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a>.
    </li>
    <li>The operator interface at <code>/</code>, embedded in the binary.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One port is a deployment decision rather than a shortcut. A collector, a
    database and a UI is three things to run, three things to upgrade, and three
    places a trace can be lost between.
  </p>

  <SourceBuild class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>Built to stay out of your way</h2>
  <p>
    Each choice below means you never change your agent to be observed.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your code calls the model.</strong> mcpd holds a run between steps
      and leaves each step to your code. Your code calls a model the same way
      before and after you adopt mcpd. The daemon's own model calls, such as a
      judged scorer, are optional. They run offline over stored traces, are opt
      in and off by default, and need an endpoint, a model and a key that you
      give it. See <a href="/docs/testing" class="link">Regression testing</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Outside the request path.</strong> Nothing your users see passes
      through mcpd.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quiet by default.</strong> There is no telemetry, no licence check
      and no vendor endpoint. The only outbound traffic is what you configure: a
      <code>--forward-endpoint</code>, a monitor's webhook, or a judge model.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Removable at any time.</strong> Remove mcpd and only the recording
      stops.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The six signals</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd stores six kinds of thing, and stores them together. Spans are what
    ran. Logs are the lines a run emitted. Metrics are numbers over time. Scores
    are a machine's judgement of a run. Feedback is a person's. An aspect note
    is one sentence a model wrote about a run once it had finished.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keeping them in one store is the whole point. Every signal carries the trace
    id and span id it belongs to, so the question "what did the model actually
    say in the run this customer complained about, and what did it cost" is one
    query rather than four tools and a timestamp you have to match by eye.
  </p>

  <SignalFlow class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    Scores and feedback are kept apart on purpose. A scorer's 0.8 and a person's
    thumbs-down measure different things, and averaging them together produces a
    number that means nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a daemon and not a framework</h2>
  <p>
    Framework observability is good, and it arrives attached to the framework.
    To be seen, an agent is expected to have been built with it, usually in one
    language. That is a fair trade for a new project and an impossible one for
    an agent that already exists and already works. Framework workflows are the
    same trade: durable, as long as every step is written in the framework's
    language and runs inside its process.
  </p>
  <p>
    A daemon inverts both. A workflow's graph is data the daemon holds; its
    step bodies are code your process holds, claimed one lease at a time
    through an HTTP protocol any language can speak. Nowhere in that exchange
    does the daemon see a model, a prompt or a credential. See
    <a href="/docs/workflows" class="link">Workflows</a> and
    <a href="/docs/workers" class="link">Workers</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A daemon inverts the trade. mcpd speaks OTLP, which is a protocol rather
    than a dependency, so it records agents written in any language, with any
    framework, including ones written before mcpd existed. If your agent already
    exports OpenTelemetry, pointing
    <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code> at mcpd is the entire integration.
  </p>
  <p>
    The SDKs exist because OTLP has no vocabulary for a prompt, a completion, a
    token count or a score, and those are the fields you came for. Using one is
    a choice, not a condition. All six behave identically: recording a span
    appends to a bounded queue and returns, network work happens on a background
    worker, and nothing in the instrumentation path ever throws. Observability
    that can take down the system it observes is worse than none.
  </p>

  <h2>One binary, two deployments</h2>
  <p>
    The container you run on a laptop is the container that runs as a Deployment
    on someone's cluster. Retention, redaction, sampling and API keys are flags
    on that same binary, not a different build and not a licence tier. There is
    no feature that exists only in a version you cannot have.
  </p>
  <p>
    The defaults are set for the laptop, which means ingest and reads are open
    when no keys are configured. mcpd warns loudly at startup when it is
    listening on a non-loopback address in that state. It starts anyway, because
    refusing to would break the five-minute path for the person it is aimed at,
    but silence would be irresponsible.
  </p>

